768e35c7f0858936f5e17379381c00dd.png)\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n![](images/11/1153c649732a1fad021e0118b5779e55f3b18de5958479088343770d1b8f95bc.png)\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n![](images/43/4369f0ba7e84c6e3a9fc540b1395fdb74ee74eb50d895fac0428dfb9cd8abe27.png)" 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186221181 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d4/d422a2e5dc28d7f3163879aa16f212b3573e9f2eb20f6aa1dd28ce2e8b55466b.svg) and \n![](images/2d/2df6f3b3a0c55d34c93291a33fca3f3f33f61a7491beb4cc495540a0f5ab5ae6.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a59de210f69e55b9de475da491dc1de9f32ccad7253ab1e4ab70714b96210e6d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/28/2832518b9ff9a0795b8b1f337a92fbd97b8b1628da52ee88a2a9c88d95c330bc.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/0e/0e9e15cf4c6eb8e07e42b3c32fffe458c230511e50e02650f8c9bf4226801b5a.svg) to get\n\n![](images/cf/cf167eaf02d0500310e31db9b3db546c3dcd1b395438bdf5daaeefec2af500ae.svg)\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n![](images/39/39b57ee5a860cfd3b73c64bebf93a77a193339530ae6970f931972a2e345a1df.svg)\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n![](images/3c/3c80deae8395322a7abb6e106ccf084428b93e00e9b8087f53aa0c8bb76c00e4.svg)" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221182 1071 "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221182 1071 "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186221182 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/58/58d027aeb94fddf42231a86a73de4be306fa6ea22ea7767dbe8a461e91a6f446.png) and \n![](images/bb/bb9297479f2149dfca76e02fb9e314205063fd3a3f967d70eee24235970206b1.png)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ea02cc0d71b67f061adb67ee344cc5474b71e1e34db122b942d2e0ed1531ae8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/8f/8f1e68dd09715b24f0de6c79fdf07af6e9810741e1a6065637db5301cd86ebe0.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/ef/ef4a060144a80ac9ecb94a71c407d1537283ecc217ac26ab053e9b8d2fad73e9.png) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)-(0)(0) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\bbox[4pt, border