h opposite signs, our equilibrium is an ![](images/4e/4e3952dffb6f2a5cd730e0f013eb0b49f49ce3be9a2e83674272991baaf57225.png)" 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186221183 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n![](images/f8/f876ff09a7b8c0bfd16d6597389967749eeade8305ada19fe4770d575705c601.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f6/f60e24b209fce8d306850ca088ebbc091fb9ddd11cda74e46ac7e2dc9cc61610.svg) and \n![](images/57/572bf982c2dfc04ad3b0771d666e9956b5e316e9148574c53788fc66ca9cfd35.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of ![](images/41/41df3ddfc93bfc6b75f58565e44fde8667b575eab70d34a6ad26f0fc7d7b9f00.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/ea/eacac468e4ce8b3ea07d93ddfb7f6336d8a3f6a822bedaffa07cd5431aa31a1d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n![](images/8a/8aac1cd3e1f495a9f509442a8599882e61092618f03e43431d68b7e807d65c69.svg)\n\nThus, we have:\n\n![](images/83/83cdeef9e17081a8236ac8d14ea9d8b8fda3da046d95f84fadbbf93d72a9bc0a.svg)\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/81/81720438d92024448a479c3488e0478f5de9a4b26c59544fa738ca2cf6c1e6d7.svg) to get\n\n![](images/1b/1b42df3f7cf42a954e20ade84aeb884235bf693c1c03258ed316f4b13cbc01e6.svg)\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ![](images/00/009ee734f37f8813ce80ea9e89d4156cb498825273cf0f21e52818218ea99434.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n![](images/d4/d4a328abcc87b1a35120504bb31e047476aa18af01cef1498964d2a4b777f201.svg)\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues\n![](images/74/74dd38ba634c0a0d0150d29c4bc21320be4372679610877f8dab85b4dedc9182.svg)\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an ![](images/92/921a21d96fcb849ac36e1369ed9798e1c895e2549aa9e3643da1198d8403d556.svg)" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221417 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444534 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221417 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444537 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444533 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444536 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444541 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444542 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444535 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444538 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444539 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444540 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444543 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186221470 1050 1140.0 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186222005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220704 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220709 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222005 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186222005 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222494 1064 "Consider an autonomous system written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/60/60be83e58a72728b03ebfa2de6999ec80f17ee29e3f2ab3060d8ca43bbf45de8.svg) and ![](images/98/98bb67f212fa8deee63da1b69ae93086b9eb56a3ec64d70ba36e6a94dff1527b.svg).\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n![](images/e6/e640b7ca9ddc8252223ec70f011147a702cd25bca0ee8a20314693695c1bec40.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/93/93058061ca42daa7114b615bcb77a8f9ccbc9cdccec98559db3eb288a7208940.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/72/72feeca197610c24c70b01f4c1075c75eebe89ae29f1aced83caeb252c275cce.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186222494 1064 "Consider an autonomous system written as $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),$$ where $\\mathbf{x}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})=[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}(x,y) \\\\ {f}_{2}(x,y)\\end{aligned}]$.\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e. $$\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.$$\n\nFor example, let $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}), \\qquad \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})= [\\begin{aligned}x+y+{y}^{2} \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}$$ Thus, we have\n\n$$J = |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2y \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222495 1064 "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nand assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If \n\n$$\nJ=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\ndenotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$ \n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186222495 1064 "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),$$ and assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If $$J=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)$$ denotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$\n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222496 1064 "Suppose the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nis almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nSmall values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/7a/7a0ff677dae0e51e34aed63faadfcb31f277f060c03488fdc1828cf8a2a5a0ec.svg)\nand evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.\n$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186222496 1064 "Suppose the system $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),$$ is almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by $$\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$$ Small values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix $$\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})= [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}],$$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system** $$\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222497 1064 "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case:\n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary.\n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [17592186222497 1064 "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case: \n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary. \n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186368108 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186368108 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 false] [1