quilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{6}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444504 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444513 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444520 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444521 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1069 "q-335146" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1072 #uuid "ba7562c9-dc1e-5d77-b34b-c5d59c44924f" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444541 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-7x \\\\ {x}^{2}+6y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-7x)=-7,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-7x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+6y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+6y)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2y \\\\ 2x & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444503 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444505 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444506 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444511 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1069 "q-336902" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-4x(t)+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1072 #uuid "e8e22bce-e115-5d92-9ec4-991287c11960" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444542 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+y-4x+4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=2x+3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=-2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & -2y+2 \\\\ 2x-4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)+3 & -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(1)-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444522 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444523 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444543 1069 "q-336934" 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534926 17592186045417 true] [17592186444543 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)+x(t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139534926 17592186